      "location": "Definition",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "The definition requires only a finitely branching rooted tree. Its cited dependency defines this as a prefix-closed set of finite sequences with finitely many successors per node, explicitly allowing infinite branches. Let G have variables {S,A}, terminals {a}, and productions S->aA and A->A. The tree whose root has children a and A, with an infinite unary A-chain below A, satisfies every listed parse-tree condition and has terminal-leaf yield a. But G cannot derive a, since A never terminates. Thus the definition admits an infinite non-derivation as a parse tree; it needs finiteness or well-foundedness.",
      "severity": "fatal",
